#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# join_on

#% text
# `join_on(a, b, key)` gives the dataset of the rows of `a` and `b` whose column `key` agrees, one row for each matching pair.

#% text
# `join_on(a, b, [k1, k2, ...])` matches on several columns.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The key columns come first, then the other columns of `a`, then those of `b`; a name of `b` that `a` also has gets the suffix _right.

#% item
# Rows come in the order of `a`, partners in the order of `b`.

#% item
# A missing key matches nothing.

#% item
# The option `how` keeps rows without a partner: `inner` (the default) none, `left` those of `a`, `right` those of `b`, `outer` both; their other cells are missing.

#% item
# `join(a, b)` stacks the rows of `a` and `b` instead.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
pets = dataset([["owner", "pet"], ["Ana", "cat"], ["Bo", "dog"], ["Ana", "fish"]])
homes = dataset([["owner", "town"], ["Ana", "Lyon"], ["Cy", "Nice"]])
join_on(pets, homes, "owner")
#% output
#= owner | pet  | town
#= ------+------+-----
#= Ana   | cat  | Lyon
#= Ana   | fish | Lyon
#= dataset: 2 rows, 3 columns

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Keeping every pet:

#% cell
join_on(pets, homes, "owner", how: left)
#% output
#= owner | pet  | town
#= ------+------+--------
#= Ana   | cat  | Lyon
#= Bo    | dog  | missing
#= Ana   | fish | Lyon
#= dataset: 3 rows, 3 columns

#% text
# Keeping every row of both:

#% cell
join_on(pets, homes, "owner", how: outer)
#% output
#= owner | pet     | town
#= ------+---------+--------
#= Ana   | cat     | Lyon
#= Bo    | dog     | missing
#= Ana   | fish    | Lyon
#= Cy    | missing | Nice
#= dataset: 4 rows, 3 columns

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Both datasets need the key column:

#% cell
join_on(pets, homes, "pet")
#% output
#! failure(missing_key, "pet")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `join`, `dataset`, `aggregate`
